    "id": "collision_comparison",
    "prerequisites": ["restitution_drop"],
    "suggested_order": 230,
    "summary": "The top row is elastic (e=1), the middle row is inelastic (e=0), and the bottom row is partly elastic (e=0.5). Each isolated pair starts with a 1 kg disc moving right at 2 m/s and a 2 kg disc moving left at 1 m/s. All three pairs conserve momentum. How much kinetic energy they keep depends on restitution. At zero restitution, the discs leave contact with the same velocity but remain separate objects.",
    "title": "Three kinds of collision"
  },
  "version": {
    "major": 1,
    "minor": 1
  },
  "guide": {"focus":"Compare momentum and kinetic energy across elastic, inelastic and partly elastic impacts.","predict":{"prompt":"Which row keeps the most kinetic energy?","options":["Elastic top row","Inelastic middle row","Partly elastic bottom row"]},"variables":[{"control":"object.properties.mass","label":"Elastic left mass","body":"body-0-1"},{"control":"object.motion.velocity_x","label":"Elastic left incoming speed","body":"body-0-1"}],"steps":[{"text":"Change the elastic left disc's mass or incoming velocity, then press R.","control":"object.properties.mass","body":"body-0-1"},{"text":"Run to the impact and inspect its before-and-after momentum.","open":"measure.collisions.list"},{"text":"Open Theory checks and run Collisions vs theory to compare a reference collision with its theoretical result.","open":"measure.theory.collisions_run"}],"expect":"Every isolated pair conserves momentum. Only the elastic pair keeps all its kinetic energy. At zero restitution, both discs leave contact with the same velocity and remain separate objects.","limits":"The theory check has its own fixed inputs. Changes to this experiment do not affect its result."},
